mechanisms regulating embryonic development. Given the inherent ethical and technical limitations associated with the use of human embryos, studies had at first to rely almost exclusively on surrogate animal models, and in­ sights into human-specific mechanisms of development were virtually nonexistent. Nevertheless, several con­ secutive technical and scientific breakthroughs have, over the last few decades, changed this perspective. Among those, the development of robust protocols for the in vitro fertilization and culture of human blastocysts [3,4] allowed scientists to have a source of biological material suitable for research [5•,6]. Similarly, the availability of murine and human blastocysts ultimately allowed the derivation of stable pluripotent stem cell lines (PSCs) [1,2,7] that were used extensively to study the cellular mechanisms gov­ erning cell commitment during embryo development, first in 2D and then in 3D using cellular aggregates such as embryoid bodies [8]. This transition from 2D to 3D was an attempt to recapitulate the complex cellular interactions occurring during early embryonic development and driven by the idea that PSCs under appropriate culture condi­ tions, could potentially be coerced to self-organize into organ- or even embryo-like structures, which could initiate and recapitulate in vitro the morphogenetic processes as­ sociated with early development. Likely inspired and en­ couraged by the successful organ- and tissue-specific partial reconstitution of embryonic development in vitro, as shown by the plethora of work on organoids and PSC-based in vitro models of organogenesis during the last two decades, the idea of reconstituting embryonic development in toto gained momentum and was concretized using a ‘puzzlelike’ approach where basic cellular lineages normally found in a blastocyst, that is, mouse embryonic stem cells (mESCs) and trophoblast stem cells (mTSCs), were as­ sembled together in vitro to generate an embryo-like structure. The resulting ‘blastoids’ or ‘ETS’ (embryo-like structure derived from (mouse) embryonic stem cells (mESCs) and trophoblast stem cells (mTSCs)) re­ capitulated morphological features of E3.5 embryos and E5 mouse embryos, respectively [9••,10], and could replicate some aspects of the implantation process, including de­ cidualization and blood-vessel recruitment upon transfer in utero [9••]. Following these initial seminal publications, additional types of embryo-like structures were generated using a similar approach, each aiming to increase the functional relevance of the extraembryonic compartment either by adding extraembryonic endoderm cells along with mESCs and trophoblast cells, which resulted in structures called ETX embryos [11], or by using extended pluripotent stem cells (EPSCs), believed at that time to have both embryonic and extraembryonic potential, to generate EPS blastoids [12,13]. The resulting ETX (em­ bryo-like structure derived from (mouse) embryonic stem cells (mESCs), trophoblast stem cells (mTSCs)) and ex­ traembryonic endoderm presented features of $\mathrm{E6.75~em^{-}}$ bryos, including a functional regionalization of the visceral endoderm into anterior visceral endoderm along with the presence of mesodermal and definitive endodermal cells. Similarly, EPS blastoids showed characteristics of latestage mouse blastocysts at E4.5 with a segregation of the pluripotent inner cell mass (ICM)-like cells into the epi­ blast and primitive endoderm lineages. Besides mouse, EPS blastoids with similar features have also been suc­ cessfully generated using human cells [14•].
Most embryo-like in vitro models reported so far are generated by assembling the different elementary building blocks present in an embryo. Each of these blocks are represented by a stably in vitro-cultured stem cell line, with the assembled in vitro embryo model re­ capitulating at least some hallmarks and characteristics of an in vivo embryo at a given time of development. Although this strategy has yielded invaluable insights, it has until very recently failed to generate structures that could develop further and grow similarly to in vivo em­ bryos. One reason for that resides in an inherent flaw of this ‘puzzle-like’ approach as the types and features of each used cell line, which are initially assembled, have a dramatic influence on the formation efficiency and re­ capitulation potential of the resulting embryo-like struc­ tures [15]. This has been confirmed by a recent study, which suggested that some cell types assembled to gen­ erate blastoids were wrongly labeled and did not have the expected developmental potential [16–18]. To overcome this pitfall, based on the numerous reports confirming that naive PSCs have the inherent ability to generate extraembryonic lineages [19–22], an alternative embryo-like structure-generation strategy that uses naive PSCs as the sole starting material has been successfully implemented. The resulting blastoids contained both trophoblast stem cells (TSCs) and extraembryonic endoderm cells along with pluripotent ICM-like cells. These naive PSC-derived blastoids could initiate blastocoel formation, and are cur­ rently considered to be embryo-like models most similar to pre-implantation embryos [23–25]. The latest blastoids generated using this approach were even shown to be able to recapitulate embryo implantation in vitro as the tro­ phectoderm (TE) epithelium adjacent to the ICM could initiate attachment to the open surface of 2D-cultured receptive human endometrial organoids. Even more im­ pressive, after 2 days of culture, the pregnancy-related gonadotropin- $\cdot\upbeta$ hormone $(\mathrm{CG}\upbeta)$ was detected in the cul­ ture medium [24•].
Based on these latest results, we can predict that the next challenge in the field will be to push the development of these models even further and reach post-gastrulation stages of development. Considering that proper recapitulation of implantation is largely thought to be important to reach this goal and given the rapid advances on pre- and peri-implantation embryolike model systems, the ISSCR has recently published new guidelines regarding in vitro model systems of human embryonic development. The most important change in this context was the suggestion to abrogate the 14-day rule, which limits the timeframe iVF-derived blastocysts can be cultured in vitro. The further devel­ opment of human-embryo models that have at least theoretically the potential to develop into a viable or­ ganism can be studied but need to follow a thorough evaluation process with proper scientific and regulatory oversight. Moreover, although nonhuman PSCs and their derivatives, including murine blastoids and similar embryo-like structures, may potentially be transferred into animal hosts, if these experiments are deemed ab­ solutely necessary and of high scientific significance and alternative approaches are not available, all bottom-up human embryonic model systems, including human blastoids, are not allowed to be transferred into the uterus of a human or animal [26•,27•]. For that reason, development of proper in vitro platforms able to re­ capitulate human-embryo implantation is becoming a critical requirement in the field.
Besides proper implantation, reaching post-implantation and post-gastrulation-stage development might require to continue improving the existing methods of blastoid generation. Based on the two previous strategies re­ ported here, we can hypothesize that generating em­ bryo-like structures starting with cells even more upstream than naive cells, including totipotent or 8Clike cells, might result not only in an increased genera­ tion efficiency and overall lower variability, but possibly also increase the developmental potential of the re­ sulting blastoids [28–30]. Nevertheless, additional work will be needed in order to test this hypothesis as de­ scription of these cells is very recent and problems might arise due to incorrect characterization of their develop­ mental potential as seen previously for EPSCs [16–18]. Variations in both the induction and maintenance pro­ tocols might also impact the potential of these new cell types to generate proper blastoid structures as described for naive PSCs [23,24•].
## In vitro reconstitution of gastrulation and post-gastrulation development
The above-mentioned PSC-based blastoid-model sys­ tems of pre- and peri-implantation development were so far believed to lack the ability to reconstitute proper gastrulation and post-gastrulation development. Gastrulation is a fundamental developmental event during which the three principal germ layers emerge from the pluripotent epiblast and are specified in a spatially and temporally ordered manner, allowing the further coordinated development of the embryo, in­ cluding complex morphogenesis and organogenesis. Recently, a number of nonblastoid PSC-based 2D and 3D model systems have been established and are in­ creasingly used to recapitulate specific aspects of gas­ trulation and post-gastrulation-related developmental processes.
A 2D model of geometrically confined PSC colonies was shown to be able to recapitulate the self-organization of the different germ layers in reproducible 2D concentric patterns, with an outer TE-like ring, an intermediate ring of mesoderm and endoderm-like cells, and an inner circle of primitive ectoderm cells [31•]. The robust and reproducible emergence of these concentric patterns in response to a uniform BMP4 signal was linked to the establishment of a graded Activin and Nodal signaling relay dependent on the size and epithelial status of the PSC colonies [32,33].
Besides these 2D models of gastrulation, murine PSCbased 3D models of gastrulation, termed gastruloids, have also been established. Gastruloids, which were generated using a transient pulse of Wnt activation, were reported to recapitulate distinct aspects of murine postimplantation embryonic development, including sym­ metry breaking, anterior-to-posterior embryonic axis-re­ lated polarized gene expression, gastrulation-like cellular migration, and axial elongation [34,35]. Gastruloids could also be established using human PSCs, and provided scientists with a valuable tool to study distinct aspects of early human development, including sequential spatio­ temporal expression of different Hox clusters [36,37••].
Gastruloids could be further used to mimic and recon­ stitute aspects of axial development and somitogenesis, especially after exposure to Matrigel, including spatio­ temporally controlled expression of Tbx6 and Hes7, marking presomitic mesoderm as well as rostrocaudal patterned expression of Uncx4.1 and Tbx18 in their respective somitic mesoderm regions. Murine PSC-de­ rived gastruloids embedded into Matrigel, termed trunklike structures, were also reported to show apicobasally polarized Sox1, Pax6, and Irx3-positive neural tube-like structures and Tbx6, Msgn1, and Hes7-positive pre­ somitic mesoderm and epithelial somite-like structures derived thereof [38,39•]. Similar models of axial devel­ opment were recently also reported and established using human PSCs, and used to study mechanisms as­ sociated with the somitogenesis process, including seg­ mentation clock [40], rostrocaudal patterning [41], or FGF and WNT signaling [42]. Our group also described recently a human PSC-based model of axial develop­ ment and somitogenesis, termed axioloids. Our axioloid model system reconstituted all major hallmarks of somitogenesis, including oscillatory activity of the seg­ mentation clock and coordinated sequential formation of patterned epithelial somites. In-depth assessment of our model system revealed an unexpected crucial role for retinoic acid (RA) signaling in the stabilization of the segmentation process and the formation of properly patterned epithelial somites. We furthermore used this approach, to model diseases of the spine, generating models with induced PSC containing disease-associated mutations in HES7 and MESP2 [43].
Recapitulating proper development of the central and peripheral nervous system (CNS & PNS) in vitro has proven to be challenging, with most studies using al­ ternative strategies such as PSC-derived neuromuscular organoids [44] or corticomotor assembloids [45]. Re­ cently, using the gastruloid approach, scientists managed to also recapitulate certain aspects of CNS morphogen­ esis in vitro. Inhibition of Wnt signaling resulted in anteroposterior polarized mouse gastruloids with Otx2- positive putative neuroepithelial rosettes and a gene signature characteristic of the forebrain, midbrain, and hindbrain [46]. Similarly, distinct aspects of PNS mor­ phogenesis were successfully observed in elongating multi-lineage-organized (EMLO) human gastruloids. These structures had SOX2-positive neuronal anterior domains that developed into a spinal cord-like structure showing dorsoventral patterning as assessed by the ex­ pression of Lhx9 and Nkx6.1. These neuronal structures were closely associated with a GATA6-positive posterior mesendodermal domain that developed into a primitive gut-like tubular epithelial structure [47•]. Similar elon­ gated central epithelial endodermal structures re­ miniscent of the primitive gut tube could be generated in gastruloids with a FOXA2-positive endodermal ante­ rior and TBXT-positive mesodermal posterior pole [48,49•].
Gastruloids are used to also study other complex orga­ nogenesis events, including development of the heart, which is one of the first organs to form and function in the embryo. Upon treatment with bFGF, VEGF, and ascorbic acid, mouse gastruloids could recapitulate in vitro the consecutive waves of progenitor specification that ultimately leads to functional cardiomyocyte dif­ ferentiation, including formation of an epithelial beating structure positive for Gata4 and other cardiac markers in the anterior pole of these cardiac gastruloids. Besides cellular identity, key hallmarks of cardiac morphogenesis could be observed, including formation of a cardiac crescent and segregation of cardiac progenitors into first and second heart fields marked by Tbx5 or Isl1 ex­ pression [50]. Another recently reported cardiac-gas­ truloid approach, which involved additional treatment with HGF and IGF-1, achieved even more advanced heart-like structures, including formation of a heart tube that can undergo looping and can give rise to segregated
