heart-chamber-like structures, including a putative out­ flow tract [51].
In addition to gastruloid-based models of peri- and postgastrulation embryonic development, two-component model systems, so-called embryoids, consisting of a large murine ESC body and a small artificial morphogen-sig­ naling center, have been also recently reported. These “assembled” models of post-gastrulation and post-im­ plantation embryonic development show a surprisingly high level of anterior–posterior and dorsoventral pat­ terning of all three germ- layer derivatives [52•].
Because of their inherent low complexity, when com­ pared with a full embryo, gastruloid models are be­ coming more and more a valuable tool to study and understand the mechanisms regulating the development of each individual germ layer and organ system, in­ dependent of the multiple interactions and influences from other adjacent tissues and anatomical structures of the embryo. We can expect these and similar bottom-up model systems to develop even further in the near fu­ ture, especially when combined with advanced bioen­ gineering approaches [53]. It is likely that gastruloids and related PSC-based bottom-up model systems will increasingly contribute to a better understanding of the biological principles that underlie the robust emergence of complexity during embryonic development. It can also be anticipated that gastruloids and similar 3D model systems of embryonic development will lead to the de­ velopment and utilization of more advanced and phy­ siologically relevant models of human teratogenicity, toxicology, and disease.
## Bridging pre- and post-gastrulation embryonic development in vitro
The conceptual segregation between the pre-/peri- and post-gastrulation in vitro models of embryo development is largely linked to technical and scientific limitations. Indeed, for a long time, it was not possible for scientists to bridge those two stages of embryonic development and culture a pre-implantation blastocyst up to postgastrulation stage of development. Recently, an alter­ native culture method that combines static and rotating platforms with controlled atmospheric and nutrient conditions allowed the efficient ex utero culture of E5.5 mouse pre-gastrulation embryos up to the E11 hindlimbformation stage [54•]. Using this new setup, scientists have very recently managed to also culture mouse PSCderived blastoids, generated in vitro by assembling TE, naive, and primitive endoderm cells, up to post-gas­ trulation and organogenesis stage of development [55••–57]. These so-called synthetic embryos (sEm­ bryos), have the morphogenetic characteristics of a $\mathrm{E}8.5$ mouse embryo, with the presence of a median neural tube flanked by bilateral somites, a notochord, and a posterior tail-bud structure, but also an organized ante­ rior CNS segregated into a forebrain, midbrain and hindbrain, a gut tube, and a yolk sac able to support the emergence of a hematopoietic and circulatory system that are connected to a beating heart [55••–57].
This remarkable breakthrough is a welcome and highly anticipated addition to the existing pre- and post-gas­ trulation-stage models of embryonic development. Being able to robustly and reproducibly bridge pre- and post-gastrulation development with PSC-derived sEmbryos will likely revolutionize developmental biology as we know it, and contribute to a better un­ derstanding of human embryonic development and diseases, but may also have important repercussions far beyond biology. Indeed, given the rapid pace with which this field of research on in vitro embryogenesis grows and evolves, we can surely expect similar achievements to be described for human and other primate species in the very near future. This will, without any doubt, trigger a heated debate that will encompass several spheres of the scientific, societal, and political spectrum. Recently, several countries in Europe and the United States have changed the legal framework regulating pregnancy stop [58–60]. Among the different reasons for these changes is the will to redefine the biological events used as a basis to establish the legal limit for a pregnancy stop. Several states in the United States have chosen the detection of a heartbeat as the biological event defining this limit [60,61]. This obviously raises critical concerns regarding the future of in vitro model systems of human embryogenesis, especially as human sEmbryo, when described, will most certainly have a beating heart. Calls for an urgent albeit balanced discourse on this topic, including adaptation and establishment of new ethical guidelines and bioethical laws as well as a new definition of the exact nature and ethical/moral status of sEmbryos and similar model systems, are highly warranted. Blas­ toids, gastruloids, sEmbryos, and other in vitro model systems are all valuable tools and exciting new platforms in this rapidly emerging field of research, which has the potential to transform the way and scope of how we study human biology, including human development, disease, and evolution (Figrue 1).
